{{Politics of Okaiken}}

The '''Ministry of Finance''' ({{lang-ja|財務省}}, {{Nihongo|Zaimu-shō|ざいむしょう}}) is a cabinet-level ministry of the [[Government of Okaiken]] responsible for the formulation of fiscal policy, management of the national budget, administration of the tax system, regulation of financial institutions, and issuance of sovereign debt. Widely recognized as the administrative anchor of the central bureaucracy, the ministry exercises pervasive influence over macroeconomic planning, capital formation, and inter-ministerial resource allocation.

The ministry oversees a national expenditure budget of 1.6 trillion Sen against revenues of 1.3 trillion Sen in fiscal year 2025, administering a gross public debt burden exceeding 236 percent of Gross Domestic Product. Its regulatory umbrella includes the [[Bank of Okaiken]] (the central bank), the [[Financial Services Agency (Okaiken)|Financial Services Agency]], the [[National Tax Agency (Okaiken)|National Tax Agency]], and [[Okaiken Customs]]. The incumbent Minister of Finance is [[Kazuya Fujita]] of the [[Sanese Communist Party]] (SCP), who assumed office on 20 May 2026 within the [[Ikazaki Cabinet]]. Fujita succeeded [[Satsuki Katayama]] of the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]] (PPO), following the collapse of the [[Honda-Nakayama Cabinet]].

==Historical development==

===Imperial and post-war origins===

The Ministry of Finance traces its modern lineage to the historic Ōkura-shō (大蔵省), established in 1869 during the administrative modernizations of the early restoration period. Modeled on European treasury structures, the Ōkura-shō initially unified the fragmented feudal currencies of the early modern period by inaugurating the Sanese En in 1871. Throughout the imperial period, the ministry's Budget Bureau (主計局, ''Shukei-kyoku'') operated as the supreme gatekeeper of public finances, dominating civilian cabinets and directing national capital into strategic armaments and overseas transport networks.

Following the democratic reforms of the post-war era, the ministry retained its institutional preeminence. During the Sanese Industrial Miracle from 1955 through 1973, the ministry orchestrated national industrial policy in tight coordination with the Ministry of International Trade and Industry and the Bank of Okaiken, maintaining low interest rates, rationing foreign exchange, and directing commercial bank lending toward heavy manufacturing and export shipyards. The collapse of the speculative asset bubble in 1991 severely tarnished the ministry's technocratic reputation, as prolonged economic stagnation and recurrent banking crises forced successive governments to deploy massive fiscal stimulus packages, inaugurating the modern era of elevated sovereign debt.

===The 2001 restructuring to the present===

Under the 2001 Central Government Reform, the historic Ōkura-shō was formally reconstituted as the Zaimu-shō. To curb the ministry's immense domestic concentration of power, statutory supervision of private commercial banks, insurance conglomerates, and securities exchanges was severed from the direct ministerial hierarchy and vested in the newly formed Financial Services Agency.

In contemporary politics, the ministry has stood at the center of fierce ideological debates between conservative deregulation and social redistribution. The Aoyagi administration (2014–2021) introduced the progressive Wealth Solidarity Surcharge on large estates and financial assets to fund universal healthcare and family allowances. The subsequent conservative administration of Prime Minister [[Honda Shigetsuma]] repealed this surcharge in 2022 to attract corporate capital, resulting in annual revenue shortfalls.

The fiscal architecture came under acute strain during the twin crises of early 2026: the severe currency shock of January–February 2026, which saw the En depreciate sharply against international reserve assets, and the systemic economic disruption of the [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]] in May 2026. Following the credit rating downgrade by international credit agencies in mid-2026, the newly appointed Finance Minister, Kazuya Fujita, shifted ministry policy away from neoliberal austerity toward structural tax restructuring, prioritizing windfall corporate taxes on digital monopolies, progressive capital gains revisions, and rigorous fiscal oversight.

